# keur quality control — POSIX single-bracket `[ ... ]` test in a bash script.
#
# Why this exists: SonarCloud flagged keur's own shell scripts (script:S6748 /
# "Consistency") for using the `[ ... ]` test builtin instead of the `[[ ... ]]`
# keyword. `[[` is safer and more feature-rich: it does no word-splitting or
# pathname expansion on unquoted variables, supports `&&`/`||`/`<`/`>` and `=~`
# directly, and removes a whole class of quoting bugs. This rule makes that a
# self-guarding control (fix + control, never just the fix), the same way
# KEUR-PORT-*/KEUR-QUAL-* guard the C++ side.
#
# What it flags — a `[` used as the *command* of a conditional test:
#   - a line whose first token is `[` (e.g. `[ -f x ] && ...`)
#   - `if` / `elif` / `while` / `until` immediately followed by `[ `
#   - a `[` opened right after `&&` or `||`
# It deliberately does NOT flag `[[ ... ]]` (already correct), array indexing
# (`${arr[i]}`), globs (`[abc]*`), or `[` inside a string.
#
# WARN, not block: this is a consistency smell, not a bug. It nudges toward the
# safer form without stopping a commit. Scope: bash/sh scripts only. SonarCloud
# checks test scripts too, so tests/ is NOT excluded — only vendored/build dirs.
#
# @see docs/adr/ADR-013 (self-guarding controls), SonarCloud script:S6748.
id: KEUR-QUAL-003
ilities: [analysability]
maturity: 3
title: Use `[[ ... ]]` instead of the `[ ... ]` test in bash scripts
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: pattern
action: warn
skip_comments: true
target:
  extensions: .sh .bash
  exclude_paths: vendor/ build/ dist/
patterns:
  # A `[` acting as the test command: at line start, or right after a
  # conditional keyword, or after a && / || operator. The trailing space/`]`
  # requirement avoids matching `[[`, `[abc]` globs and `${arr[i]}` indexing.
  - regex: (?:^|\bif|\belif|\bwhile|\buntil|&&|\|\|)\s*\[\s
    exclude: \[\[
    message: "POSIX `[ ... ]` test — prefer the `[[ ... ]]` keyword: no word-splitting/globbing on unquoted vars, native &&/||/</>/=~, fewer quoting bugs. @see KEUR-QUAL-003, SonarCloud script:S6748"
confidence: 0.6
fix: "Replace the `[ ... ]` test with `[[ ... ]]` in bash scripts. Note the semantics shift: inside `[[ ]]` use `==` for pattern match, `=~` for regex, and `<`/`>` for string comparison; quoting of the right-hand side of `==`/`=~` changes literal-vs-pattern behaviour. Keep `[ ]` only in strict POSIX-sh scripts that must not depend on bash."
